The dirs module
================

    >>> from pysyte.types.paths import dirs
    >>> assert 'Handle directories' in dirs.__doc__

We'll need the os module for some of the tests below

    >>> import os

Some known dirs
----------------

Root of the filesystem
    >>> assert dirs.root().isroot()

The tmp directory
    >>> assert dirs.tmp() == '/tmp'

The pwd() method gets os.getcwd() as a DirectPath

    >>> start_here = dirs.pwd()
    >>> assert start_here == os.getcwd()
    >>> assert isinstance(start_here, dirs.DirectPath)

And here() does same

    >>> assert dirs.here() == dirs.pwd()

cd()
----

The cd() method (at least) calls os.chdir()
It returns True if the change of directory is successful

    >>> assert dirs.here() == start_here
    >>> assert not dirs.cd('/no/such/path')
    >>> assert dirs.here() == start_here

Assuming a standard Unix filesystem
    >>> assert dirs.cd('/usr/lib')
    >>> assert dirs.here() == '/usr/lib'

cd() recognises the special value '-' to go back to the previous directory

    >>> assert dirs.cd('-')
    >>> assert dirs.here() == start_here

If a cd() fails the value used gets ignored for going back
    >>> dirs.cd('/no/such/path') and dirs.pwd() or 'fail'
    'fail'
    >>> dirs.cd('-') and dirs.pwd() or 'fail'
    <DirectPath '/usr/lib'>

Calling using '-' repeatedly will toggle between two directories:

    >>> assert dirs.cd('/usr')
    >>> assert dirs.cd('/opt')
    >>> for i in range(0, 4):
    ...     _ = dirs.cd('-')
    ...     print(dirs.pwd())
    ...
    /usr
    /opt
    /usr
    /opt

If the path to a file is given
    cd() will change to that file's parent directory

/bin/cp is a file on nearly all Unix systems
    but merely as a link to /usr/bin/cp on some of them

If it is a file we can cd to it
    >>> assert dirs.cd('/bin/cp')

And now expect to be in one of its known parents
    >>> assert dirs.here() in ('/bin', '/usr/bin')

